</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-triple-mass">
<title>Mass With Variable Density</title>

<example xml:id="example-triple-mass">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Assume that the density (mass per unit volume) is given by
<m>\rho(x,y,z) = x</m>. Evaluate the mass <m>M</m> of the solid
<m>R</m> of <xref ref="fig-triple-wedge-tikz"/>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
Using the mass formula <xref ref="eq-mass-triple"/> with the same
limits as in Part A of <xref ref="example-triple-order"/>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
M \amp= \int_{y=0}^{1}\!\!\int_{x=0}^{1-y}\!\!\int_{z=0}^{1-x^2}
x\,dz\,dx\,dy
= \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{1-y} x\left(1 - x^2\right)dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^1\left[\frac{x^2}{2}
- \frac{x^4}{4}\right]_{x=0}^{x=1-y} dy
= \int_0^1\left[\frac{(1-y)^2}{2}
- \frac{(1-y)^4}{4}\right]dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \left[-\frac{(1-y)^3}{6}
+ \frac{(1-y)^5}{20}\right]_0^1
= \frac16 - \frac1{20} = \frac{7}{60}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-triple-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
The first five questions all concern the same solid <m>E</m>, the region
bounded below by the paraboloid <m>z = x^2+y^2</m> and above by the upper
half <m>z = 1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m> of the sphere of radius <m>1</m>
centered at <m>(0,0,1)</m>, drawn in
<xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>. Four of them ask for a sign or
a yes-or-no answer rather than a number: the point is to read the
integrand on the region, not to integrate.
</p>

<p>
It is worth locating <m>E</m> first. The two surfaces meet where
<m>x^2+y^2 = 1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>; writing <m>u = x^2+y^2</m> this is
<m>u-1 = \sqrt{1-u}</m>, hence <m>u^2 = u</m> and <m>u = 1</m>. So
<m>E</m> sits over the unit disk <m>x^2+y^2 \le 1</m>, and for each
<m>(x,y)</m> there, <m>z</m> runs from <m>x^2+y^2</m> up to
<m>1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-triple-sign-one">
<title>The sign of an integral, without calculating</title>
<statement>
<p>
<term>Without calculation</term>, decide whether the integral below is
positive, negative, or zero. The region of integration <m>E</m> is the
region bounded by <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> and
<m>z=1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>.
</p>
<me>
\iiint_E \left(z-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}-1\right) dV
</me>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p>Positive</p></li>
<li><p>Negative</p></li>
<li><p>Zero</p></li>
</ol>
<p>
<em>Hint</em>: begin by determining the limits of integration.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<term>B</term>, negative.
</p>
<p>
The upper surface of <m>E</m> is <m>z = 1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>, so every
point of <m>E</m> satisfies
<m>z \le 1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>, that is
</p>
<me>
z-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}-1 \le 0 .
</me>
<p>
The integrand is exactly the (signed) vertical distance from a point of
<m>E</m> up to the sphere, so it is never positive, and it vanishes only
on the cap itself, a surface of zero volume. An integrand that is
negative on the interior of the solid gives a negative integral.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-triple-mass-density-one">
<title>Could it be a mass density?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Can the function <m>f(x,y,z)=z-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}-1</m> represent mass
density (mass per unit volume) over the region <m>E</m>, which is graphed
in <xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>?
</p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p>Yes</p></li>
<li><p>No</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<term>B</term>, no. Mass is never negative, so a mass density must
satisfy <m>f \ge 0</m>. As we just saw, this <m>f</m> is negative
throughout the interior of <m>E</m>. A region carrying it would have
negative mass.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-triple-charge-density">
<title>Could it be a charge density?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Can the function <m>f(x,y,z)=z-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}-1</m> represent charge
density (charge per unit volume) over the region <m>E</m>, which is
graphed in <xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>?
</p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p>Yes</p></li>
<li><p>No</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<term>A</term>, yes. Charge comes in both signs, so a charge density is
free to be negative; there is nothing wrong with a body that is
negatively charged everywhere. The triple integral of
<xref ref="q-triple-sign-one"/> is then the total charge, and its
negative sign says the body carries a net negative charge.
</p>
<p>
This is the whole difference between the two questions: the mathematics
is identical, and only the physical meaning of the sign changes.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-triple-sign-two">
<title>The sign of a second integral</title>
<statement>
<p>
<term>Without calculation</term>, decide whether the integral below is
positive, negative, or zero. The region of integration <m>E</m> is the
region bounded by <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> and
<m>z=1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>.
</p>
<me>
\iiint_E \left(2-x^2-y^2\right) dV
</me>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p>Positive</p></li>
<li><p>Negative</p></li>
<li><p>Zero</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<term>A</term>, positive.
</p>
<p>
The solid lies over the unit disk, so <m>x^2+y^2 \le 1</m> at every point
of <m>E</m>, and therefore
</p>
<me>
2-x^2-y^2 \ge 2-1 = 1 \gt 0 .
</me>
<p>
The integrand is at least <m>1</m> on all of <m>E</m>, so the integral is
positive<mdash/>in fact it is at least the volume of <m>E</m>. Note that
no antiderivative was needed: only the projection of <m>E</m> onto the
<m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-triple-mass-density-two">
<title>Could this one be a mass density?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Can the function <m>f(x,y,z)=2-x^2-y^2</m> represent mass density (mass
per unit volume) over the region <m>E</m>, which is graphed in
<xref ref="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere"/>?
</p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p>Yes</p></li>
<li><p>No</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<term>A</term>, yes. On <m>E</m> we have <m>1 \le 2-x^2-y^2 \le 2</m>, so
<m>f</m> is positive there and can be read as a density: the solid is
densest along its axis and lightest near the rim, where the two surfaces
meet. Its mass would be the integral of
<xref ref="q-triple-sign-two"/>.
</p>
<p>
Beware of the phrase <q>over the region <m>E</m></q>. The same formula
would not do on a larger region: outside the cylinder
<m>x^2+y^2 = 2</m> it turns negative.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-triple-paraboloid-sphere">
<caption>
Figure (3): the region of integration <m>E</m>, bounded below by the
paraboloid <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> and above by
<m>z=1+\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>. The two surfaces meet over the circle
<m>x^2+y^2=1</m>.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="snf5uec4"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. Turning it
until you look nearly along the <m>xy</m>-plane shows the two surfaces
closing on each other at the rim, which is where
<m>x^2+y^2=1</m>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-triple-two-cylinders">
<title>A volume between two cylinders</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a triple integral for computing the volume of the region bounded by
the two cylinders <m>y=x^2</m> and <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and the two planes
<m>z=0</m> and <m>z=\frac13(x+y+1)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-triple-two-cylinders"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The two cylinders are vertical walls standing on the curves <m>y=x^2</m>
and <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> of the <m>xy</m>-plane, and those curves cross at
<m>x=0</m> and <m>x=1</m>. Between them <m>\sqrt{x} \ge x^2</m>, so the
shadow of the solid is
</p>
<me>
D = \left\{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 1,\ x^2 \le y \le \sqrt{x}\right\} ,
</me>
<p>
which is the region of <xref ref="fig-triple-two-cylinders"/> lying
between the two walls. The floor is <m>z=0</m> and the ceiling is the
plane, so
</p>
<me>
V = \int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}}\!\!\int_{0}^{\frac13(x+y+1)}
    dz\,dy\,dx .
</me>
<p>
Carrying it out is quick, since the innermost integral just returns the
height of the ceiling:
</p>
<me>
V = \frac13\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} (x+y+1)\,dy\,dx
  = \frac{19}{90} \approx 0.211 .
</me>
</answer>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-triple-two-cylinders">
<caption>
Figure (4): the solid bounded by the cylinders <m>y=x^2</m> and
<m>y=\sqrt{x}</m>, the plane <m>z=0</m>, and the plane
<m>z=\frac13(x+y+1)</m>, whose two edges are drawn on the walls.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="af2ud86q"
             width="100%" aspect="600:600" design-width="600"
             material-width="600" material-height="600"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. Looking
straight down shows the region <m>D</m> between the two curves in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, which is the shadow the triple integral is built over.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
